# secrets and customer metadata - never commit
#
# The credential-file patterns are deliberately broader than ".env". A file named "env" (no leading
# dot) was found untracked in the working tree holding the same 125 lines of hosts and passwords as
# .env, and nothing was ignoring it - one "git add ." would have committed it. Editors, shell
# redirects and copy-paste all produce these variants, so they are all covered rather than the one
# spelling anybody remembers. ".env.example" stays committed via the negation below.
.env
env
env.*
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
profiles.yaml
profiles.local.yaml
output/
extracts/
cache/
*.sqlite
.kiro/settings/mcp.json
__pycache__/
.venv/
# Python build / tooling
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.eggs/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/

# secret-scan reports (the .secrets.baseline IS committed; ad-hoc reports are not)
.secrets.report.json

# generated knowledge base (rendered by bw_generate_docs) goes to output/, never committed
# (output/ and extracts/ are already ignored above)

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# local landscape reference (internal endpoints; never commit)
landscape.local.md

# ad-hoc probe/verification scratch scripts and their output. These run against a live system and
# routinely carry concrete object names, so they must never be committed (see SECURITY.md).
tmp_*

